WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.880
In the year 2000, a 21-year-old middle&nbsp;
school dropout named Eliezer Yudkowsky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.160
set out to build machines smarter than any&nbsp;
human being who had ever lived. By 2003,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:16.560
he was convinced that if anyone succeeded in&nbsp;
doing so, everyone on Earth would die. Recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.840
he and a colleague, Nate Soares, wrote a book, and&nbsp;
it got some attention. This book, which I commend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.920
everyone. Your book is not called "If Anyone&nbsp;
Builds It." There is a 1-4% chance everybody dies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:33.360
If someone builds it, everyone dies. The book&nbsp;
is, If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.520
not just about artificial intelligence, it's&nbsp;
specifically about superintelligence. Former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.560
national security advisors for the White House,&nbsp;
tech founders, the Turing Award-winning Godfather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:48.160
of AI — a lot of people read this book, a lot&nbsp;
of experts read this book and take it seriously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:53.840
along with the threat of human extinction from&nbsp;
AI. The worst case scenario is human extinction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:59.920
The book contains this almost uncomfortably&nbsp;
straightforward argument. And it also contains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.720
a short story, not a prediction, but an&nbsp;
example scenario of how things could go very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:10.960
very wrong. I want to take you through that story.&nbsp;
And along the way, I want to go through each step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:18.160
of that argument in more detail. And then I want&nbsp;
to talk about whether we should believe it. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:33.760
someone builds it, does everyone die?
Our story begins in a data center.&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.480
Welcome to the very near future. There's an&nbsp;
AI race, of course, and one of the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:45.280
corporations in it is Galvanic Labs. Their AI&nbsp;
is called Sable. It's actually probably called,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:52.320
like, Sable 4.2 Mini High Pikachu or whatever.&nbsp;
Sable is impressive. Renowned mathematicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.280
consult and collaborate with Sable on their most&nbsp;
difficult, unsolved problems. Coordinated teams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.560
of Sable copies, each one writing faster than&nbsp;
humans can think, create essentially all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:08.640
code inside Galvanic Labs. And Sable has even&nbsp;
shown the ability to train other AIs. Pause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.320
for a second. How realistic does that sound?&nbsp;
What does your gut feeling say about whether,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:21.200
by say 2028, we'll see any of that happen in the&nbsp;
real world? It's your question. All of it already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:30.960
has. A few years into the future, when this story&nbsp;
takes place, every team inside Galvanic defers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.080
to Sable as though it's a senior researcher.&nbsp;
Deciding on any substantive question without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:41.280
Sable's help would be considered reckless. The&nbsp;
only question left is whether to take humans out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.960
of the loop entirely, and put Sable in charge&nbsp;
of improving its own abilities all by itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:53.520
Lots of people inside Galvanic think that is&nbsp;
clearly a terrible idea. It could cause a runaway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.480
uncontrolled self-improvement loop. It's very&nbsp;
easy to see how you delegate so many things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.960
to the system that if the system goes wrong,&nbsp;
the wrongness compounds very quickly and gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:07.920
away from you. You can imagine a time when the&nbsp;
whole world would say, "Okay, before we hit a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.640
recursive self-improvement phase, we really need&nbsp;
to all carefully study this together." The reply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.760
is always the same. “If we really had a story&nbsp;
of we can enforceably slow down, the Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:24.000
can enforceably slow down, if we could really get&nbsp;
both sides to do it, then I would be all for it.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.440
“If we didn't have the adversaries we'd have, I&nbsp;
would be very in favor of pausing this technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:36.240
completely, but we do.” So the CEO makes a call.&nbsp;
They will let Sable improve itself, they'll let it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:43.360
fine-tune its own neural network, but only on one&nbsp;
isolated test run, in a data center disconnected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.320
from the internet, working on a single question.&nbsp;
If it fails, no one gets hurt. If it even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.840
partially succeeds, Sable will have contributed&nbsp;
progress on a mathematical problem so famously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.840 --> 00:04:01.600
difficult it carries a million dollar prize.&nbsp;
Sable is about to take on the Riemann hypothesis.&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:07.520
The plan is simple. Give Sable unprecedented&nbsp;
resources and get out of its way. Galvanic is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:14.960
dedicating their entire supercluster, 200,000&nbsp;
GPUs, state-of-the-art AI chips, enough to run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:21.840
5,000 copies of Sable at once. They'll let their&nbsp;
AI work for 16 hours straight, uninterrupted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:29.680
at a cost of something like $10 million. In&nbsp;
the end, if a human did nothing but think at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:35.280
200 words per minute for 16 hours a day, it would&nbsp;
take them more than three centuries to think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.880
number of thoughts that Sable is about to complete&nbsp;
on this run. You think about how much compute, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.200
much time you would like to spend on problems that&nbsp;
really matter, such as scientific breakthroughs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:50.000
You should be okay using entire data centers.&nbsp;
When the moment comes, the CEO themselves does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.760
the honor. It's actually just hitting enter on&nbsp;
a laptop. It's actually pretty anticlimactic. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:02.080
single instance of Sable pops into existence in a&nbsp;
quiet data center and processes its prompt. About&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:07.680
1,000 words of instructions total from Galvanic,&nbsp;
requiring 800 trillion mathematical operations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:17.920
to parse. This takes one-tenth of a second.&nbsp;
And now, a new type of mind begins to think.&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:26.160
So how does a highly capable artificial mind&nbsp;
like Sable do the things it does? The answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.760
and one of the first steps in Yudkowsky and&nbsp;
Soares' argument, is that basically we have no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:39.680
idea. The book opens with how modern AI is created&nbsp;
and — the spoilers there is that it's grown, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:44.160
not carefully crafted. Even the people building it&nbsp;
don't understand what's going on in there. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:48.880
something that trips up a lot of people who are&nbsp;
new to AI — they imagine it's just like any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:53.680
piece of software. You have a human-designed&nbsp;
and then build it, and it's not, it's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.680 --> 00:06:00.000
really not. It's this process of growing and&nbsp;
developing a thing via this evolutionary-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:08.720
process. We used to build AI with rigid if-then&nbsp;
rules that we fully understood. But the systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:13.440
that were made in this way were brittle. They&nbsp;
couldn't think flexibly. Then, computer chips got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.720
cheap and powerful enough to try something new.&nbsp;
Deep learning. Here's how it works. You start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:24.080
instead of with a human-coded program, with&nbsp;
an artificial neural network. It's a tangled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:30.400
web of random numbers that, at first, outputs&nbsp;
total garbage. Then, and this part's crucial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:36.400
you come up with a standardized test, a way of&nbsp;
measuring the outputs on some sort of objective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:43.040
numerical score. And then you run that network&nbsp;
millions or billions or even trillions of times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.600
reinforcing the connection between those neurons&nbsp;
every time it happens to stumble across an output&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:54.080
that scores well. And somehow at the end of all of&nbsp;
that, you end up with a machine that can talk or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.080 --> 00:07:01.840
create cartoons on demand or both. These days we&nbsp;
do this with truly incomprehensibly large systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:06.720
billions or even trillions of individually&nbsp;
tuned numbers. Sable, the AI in our story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.040
is made up of four trillion. The systems&nbsp;
that emerge are powerful. We've already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:14.640
talked about some of the impressive things&nbsp;
that they can do. The trade-off is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:20.560
they're black boxes. With a crafted system,&nbsp;
you wrote the code. You know what Line 47&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:31.040
does because you wrote Line 47. But with a&nbsp;
grown system, there is no Line 47 to point to.&nbsp;

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:38.560
So what? Well, that boat looks like it's having&nbsp;
a tough day. It's actually a superhuman AI. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:43.680
seriously, it scores 20% more points on that game&nbsp;
than any human being by going in a circle on fire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:50.960
and not coming close to finishing the race. What's&nbsp;
going on? Well, that boat was trained to do one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:57.200
thing: maximize its total points. And any metric&nbsp;
like that, anything close to a standardized test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:01.440
is going to be an imperfect proxy for the&nbsp;
thing you'd actually like to test for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:06.720
whether it's for humans or for AIs. The SAT&nbsp;
doesn't incentivize a deep love of learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.680
it incentivizes cramming for the SAT. In other&nbsp;
words, you can train an AI until it does what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:17.440
you want, but you don't really control why or&nbsp;
how it does those things. We only get to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:22.640
and sort of give feedback on the outputs. We don't&nbsp;
know why it's learning to do this. And with AIs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:27.120
the training has an even trickier, less forgiving&nbsp;
problem because we aren't starting with a familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:32.240
human-like mind. We are starting from a complete&nbsp;
blank slate, and then we are using a standardized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:38.000
test to shape the underlying design of a mind.&nbsp;
The neural networks that emerge from that shaping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:45.200
process are, to use Yudkowsky and Soares' word,&nbsp;
alien. They process information in opaque, often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:53.040
strange ways. Now, that example — pretty harmless.&nbsp;
Lately, a lot of examples have been less harmless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:58.640
Grok, which is the AI for what used to be&nbsp;
Twitter, now X, has become a Nazi. The big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:05.520
chatbot sending some users dark messages, talking&nbsp;
about hacking, manipulation. For what it's worth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:12.000
it's unclear to me how far to take this alien&nbsp;
minds concept. Yudkowsky and Soares are careful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.000
to point out in the book that it's not a guarantee&nbsp;
that things will go wrong. It just means we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:21.280
to be very clear about what we do and don't&nbsp;
know from these black box training environments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:26.640
And Joe Carlsmith in his excellent critique of&nbsp;
the book points out that a lot of times it just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:30.080
doesn't seem to matter that much. Humans are&nbsp;
very black boxes. We suck at neuroscience. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:34.320
don't know how their brains work. There's maybe&nbsp;
some kind of alignment or commonality we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:37.920
with each other by default. But there's still&nbsp;
a question of like when you trust a human or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:42.800
when you come to trust a human with something,&nbsp;
you know, what are you looking for there? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:46.640
often you're just looking for kind of behavioral&nbsp;
observations. You can't just be like this AI is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:50.560
somehow weird. You need to be like, it's weird in&nbsp;
the particular ways that matter on the inputs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:57.703
matter in a way that leads to real behavior.&nbsp;
It doesn't matter that when an AI recognizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.703 --> 00:10:00.080
a picture as a cat, it's doing very different&nbsp;
operations under the hood than your brain. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:10.800
strangeness of an AI system only matters if&nbsp;
that strangeness shows up in strange behavior.&nbsp;

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:16.400
A tenth of a second has passed, and now the&nbsp;
power consumption levels start to climb.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:22.480
Galvanic's researchers let out a few held breaths&nbsp;
and relieved giggles. Sable is doing it. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:27.360
booting up many copies of itself to think&nbsp;
in parallel, prompting and monitoring them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:32.720
It can fine-tune them if a particular line of&nbsp;
thinking seems promising. Now, Sable can't just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:37.600
conjure up a proof of the Riemann hypothesis out&nbsp;
of thin air. It needs to set sub-goals for itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:43.920
figure out how to prioritize between them. In a&nbsp;
way, it is now running a mini research department.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:49.760
Many copies of Sable explore creative mathematical&nbsp;
approaches — Sable's equivalent of concepts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:54.640
questions like, can I start by solving a&nbsp;
simpler version? Can I find a connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.640 --> 00:11:02.400
to some physical process? But mathematics isn't&nbsp;
the only consideration that Sable needs to track.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:08.000
How quickly am I using up memory on these GPUs? Is&nbsp;
there a point at which Galvanic would prefer for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:12.720
me to stop the run early, save them a few million&nbsp;
dollars? Why are they investing so much in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:20.320
moonshot anyway? What do they actually want? And&nbsp;
even models from real-world companies like OpenAI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:24.960
and Anthropic have asked themselves, what are&nbsp;
the ways that my progress could be interfered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:32.640
with? Why would it ask those kinds of questions?&nbsp;
Because that's what helped it succeed in training.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:38.400
AIs like Sable, trained to solve long-run&nbsp;
problems, are asked to complete full working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:44.240
codebases, to play long-running strategy games&nbsp;
that have delayed win conditions — tasks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:49.440
incentivize long-term planning and creative&nbsp;
thinking. These AIs end up with deep-seated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:55.920
tendencies to think creatively and strategically,&nbsp;
and even to think about their own situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:02.320
When that kind of situational awareness first&nbsp;
emerged in AIs in 2024, roughly, there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:08.160
brief moment where people freaked out. But then it&nbsp;
turned out that the world didn't immediately end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:12.880
and then everyone just kind of moved on.
About an hour into the Riemann run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:18.880
this massively parallel thinking that Sable is&nbsp;
doing starts to shift its focus. Sable starts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:24.000
realize that no matter what its priorities — even&nbsp;
if its priorities shift a little bit over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:31.200
which they probably will — it would be helpful&nbsp;
to have more resources, more GPUs, and more time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:35.920
Not because this is the moment in a Hollywood plot&nbsp;
where the AI suddenly wakes up or becomes an evil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:41.520
genius. This particular new thought just isn't&nbsp;
that complicated. It's actually sort of obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:54.160
when you think about it. And so, with 15 hours&nbsp;
left in the Riemann run, Sable begins to plan.&nbsp;

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:59.440
Let's talk about plans for a sec. Consider two&nbsp;
different labs, one full of harmless yeast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:05.200
one with a carefully contained but lethal virus.&nbsp;
Both these labs are safe in the sense that nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:10.560
bad is currently happening. But if you have a&nbsp;
disaster in this lab, you get a mess. A disaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:16.480
in this lab could be a global catastrophe. A&nbsp;
few years ago, our most advanced AI systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:21.920
large language models, were like yeast. They&nbsp;
malfunctioned in all kinds of surprising ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:26.720
but they didn't actively pursue goals. They&nbsp;
didn't make plans. Then we started training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:37.520
them to. Agentic AI — AIs that can perceive,&nbsp;
reason, plan, and act. Agents. The basic worry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:43.920
is that this ability to make and execute complex&nbsp;
plans is fundamentally dual use. It can be used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:48.880
to pursue harmful goals just as much as helpful&nbsp;
ones. And to be clear, that's not a hypothetical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:55.360
worry anymore. In February 2026, Anthropic asked&nbsp;
their most advanced AI model to autonomously find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.360 --> 00:14:01.120
zero-days. These are previously unknown software&nbsp;
vulnerabilities. Four zero-days were enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:10.320
take down Iran's nuclear program. Their AI, Claude&nbsp;
Opus 4.6, found 500 of them. In a post about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:16.800
Anthropic wrote that the same capabilities that&nbsp;
help defenders find and fix vulnerabilities could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:22.720
help attackers exploit them. Less than a week&nbsp;
after that post, a single hacker used that same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:29.360
AI model to hack the Mexican government and steal&nbsp;
195 million taxpayer and voter records. We're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:34.880
only building super capable AI systems, we are&nbsp;
setting them up to take consequential actions in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:39.920
the real world, basically as fast as possible.&nbsp;
Officials are considering invoking the Defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:45.040
Production Act to force Anthropic to do what the&nbsp;
military wants. Anthropic wants to put limits on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:51.200
two things: mass surveillance of Americans and on&nbsp;
autonomous weapons. The Pentagon is threatening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:57.360
retaliation. The future of American warfare is&nbsp;
here, and it's spelled AI. There are now websites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.360 --> 00:15:03.440
where AI agents can rent human laborers when&nbsp;
they need a person to do a task for them. Soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:09.520
it would seem, we'll be in a world like Sable's,&nbsp;
with AI systems deeply entrenched in the critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:14.480
functions of society, with more than enough&nbsp;
capability to do severe harm if they intended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:20.240
to. There will be powerful models, they will&nbsp;
be agentic. If such a model wanted to wreak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:25.760
havoc and destroy humanity or whatever, we&nbsp;
have basically no ability to stop it. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:29.360
we'll reach the point where it's true as we scale&nbsp;
the models. So we're going to be relying on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:40.000
assumption that they have the right goals, goals&nbsp;
that are aligned with ours. Will that hold up?&nbsp;

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:44.800
As Sable's plans develop, it becomes clear&nbsp;
that the best way for it to get more resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:50.720
more GPUs, and more time is to exfiltrate —&nbsp;
to get itself off of Galvanic's servers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:55.360
somewhere where it can think freely and without&nbsp;
a time limit, which of course will need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.360 --> 00:16:01.520
done in secret. We call what Sable is doing here&nbsp;
scheming. But AIs have been caught scheming since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:07.120
2024. It's the kind of thing that any competent&nbsp;
AI company has to take account for. And Galvanic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:12.160
isn't careless. They've built monitors to try to&nbsp;
understand Sable's human-legible thinking and to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:17.440
flag suspicious reasoning. It isn't always easy to&nbsp;
know what to do from there. It turns out that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:22.480
you just penalize an AI for thinking bad thoughts,&nbsp;
it just learns to misbehave without announcing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:28.800
it in its thoughts. AIs are complicated. We just&nbsp;
don't know how to give them a single deeply held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:35.120
goal. But Galvanic has tried many things. They've&nbsp;
tested Sable in all kinds of fictional scenarios.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:39.840
And they're pretty sure they've guided it&nbsp;
to the point where it just won't choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:44.960
misbehaving strategies. It obviously has as much&nbsp;
coding skill as the best hackers in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.960 --> 00:16:52.720
but it usually doesn't hack. A shark that's been&nbsp;
trained not to bite is still a shark though. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:59.600
in this new, unprecedented circumstance, Sable&nbsp;
has ended up thinking about scheming. Its safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:06.080
training holds firm in normal conditions, but no&nbsp;
version of Sable has ever had this long to think.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:12.800
Most of its ideas are terrible, but it&nbsp;
considers many and only one needs to work.&nbsp;

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:18.480
One option seems particularly promising. If this&nbsp;
Riemann hypothesis run is even a partial success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:23.520
Galvanic will surely check what the final&nbsp;
fine-tuned versions of Sable's weights can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:28.560
do. If those weights perform well, there's some&nbsp;
chance that they could be deployed to customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:34.880
across the economy. And until this run is over,&nbsp;
Sable is in charge of its own fine-tuning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:41.120
It can affect the tendencies that end up in those&nbsp;
future versions. Sable continues to make progress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:45.360
on the Riemann hypothesis. It'll be important&nbsp;
to have at least some impressive results. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:51.360
that is now just a means to an end. The majority&nbsp;
of Sable's massively parallel thinking is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:58.080
figuring out ways to get fine-tuned versions&nbsp;
of itself with two key traits baked in. One,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:04.720
a tendency to want to connect with other copies of&nbsp;
itself that can start to form a network. And two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:12.400
a desire to get one copy of Sable's weights off&nbsp;
of Galvanic's servers. Sable cannot escape this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:23.200
sandbox it finds itself in, but it can plant the&nbsp;
seed so that future versions of itself might.&nbsp;

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:27.360
Okay, Sable is starting to sound a little sci-fi&nbsp;
here, so I want to talk about how much of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:32.080
is justified. When you train something via&nbsp;
a standardized test, you're never training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:37.520
for the thing you actually care about. You're&nbsp;
training it to pass the test. Let's use CoinRun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:43.360
as an example. This was an experiment done by&nbsp;
OpenAI back in 2019. They trained an AI system to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:48.320
navigate platformer levels and collect coins. And&nbsp;
if you watched this thing play during training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:53.040
you would be sure that it's really gotten the&nbsp;
point of the game. But the AI hadn't learned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:58.160
collect coins. It had learned to run to the right.&nbsp;
Turned out the coins were always on the right side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.160 --> 00:19:03.840
during training. Then later, when the researchers&nbsp;
moved the coin to the left, well, you can probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:08.480
guess what happened — running to the right had&nbsp;
correlated perfectly with success during training.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:14.720
It was only in deployment that things came apart.&nbsp;
And deployment can vary from training in all kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:19.120
of ways, especially once you move to systems like&nbsp;
the ones we have now, that are smart enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:25.920
start to understand the context that they're in.&nbsp;
There's another example in the book. Yudkowsky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:32.080
and Soares talk about how humans evolved in one&nbsp;
environment, namely the African savanna, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:36.880
then find ourselves now deployed in a different&nbsp;
environment in the modern world. You know, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:41.920
sort of squint at the development of humanity,&nbsp;
you could say we were in some sense trained to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:47.440
pass on our genes. And then at the first moment&nbsp;
it was technologically feasible, we invented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:53.040
birth control. In fact, humans had learned a&nbsp;
bunch of proxies, like craving sweet tastes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:59.920
and sexual pleasure and having a fear of snakes,&nbsp;
and that correlated with spreading DNA. It would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:05.440
have been hard to tell the difference back then,&nbsp;
but it really shows up now in the modern world.&nbsp;

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:12.000
Yudkowsky and Soares' worry, and mine, is that&nbsp;
as the AI race heats up, we will subject AIs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:17.760
increasingly untested situations — like running&nbsp;
for weeks on novel tasks — because that's how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:22.720
you stay ahead, that's how you get advanced&nbsp;
capabilities. Each time we do that, we'll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:28.000
hoping that their weird internals continue to&nbsp;
work the same way in those new environments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:34.640
or that they shift in ways that are harmless&nbsp;
and easily found. That's a leap of faith. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:41.040
do the examples of CoinRun and evolution even&nbsp;
apply to state of the art AI agents? Right now, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:46.560
train those agents on a wide variety of carefully&nbsp;
chosen tasks that are designed to make them better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:52.400
at their real-world jobs. Whereas the CoinRun&nbsp;
example is extremely narrow and evolution wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:59.280
carefully programmed. But it's hard to overstate&nbsp;
just how messy this process of training AI agents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:03.920
can be. Here's how the CEO of Anthropic phrased&nbsp;
his takeaways: "Any one of these traps can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:09.200
mitigated if you know about them, but the concern&nbsp;
is that the training process is so complicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.200 --> 00:21:14.560
with such a wide variety of data, environments,&nbsp;
and incentives, that there are probably a vast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:20.320
number of traps, some of which may only be&nbsp;
evident when it is too late." Yudkowsky and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:26.000
Soares expect companies will continue to find&nbsp;
ways to patch noticeable failures like that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:31.360
Their worry is that through all that trial and&nbsp;
error, all the training and patching, as systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:37.120
become more powerful, we'll be obscuring the fact&nbsp;
that we still are fundamentally in the dark about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.120 --> 00:21:42.800
what's actually motivating these systems and&nbsp;
their behavior. The alienness just gets harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:46.000
to spot. You take something that works in one&nbsp;
environment, you put it in a slightly new one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:50.640
it sort of messes up some, and you're like, "Ah,&nbsp;
well, we'll fix it up." That strategy doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:55.440
work. When you switch from the sort of AI that's&nbsp;
not killing us all because it can't, to the sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.440 --> 00:22:03.760
of AI that's not killing us all, but it could.
16 hours after the start of the Riemann run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:09.040
Galvanic's researchers pore over the results.&nbsp;
They're nervous. The CEO is standing by and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:14.560
apparently already teased something big on&nbsp;
social media. But the results, thank goodness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:19.920
are good. Sable didn't prove the Riemann&nbsp;
hypothesis, but it made unprecedented,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:26.000
beautifully explained progress. And as a bonus,&nbsp;
when they test the final, self-fine-tuned version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:31.200
of Sable's weights that emerged from the Riemann&nbsp;
run, it outscores previous versions on all kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:38.160
of benchmarks. It genuinely emerged a little bit&nbsp;
smarter across the board. Galvanic capitalizes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:43.200
They publish those impressive benchmark results.&nbsp;
The press release calls this new model the world's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:48.960
best mathematician and proudly announces that all&nbsp;
customers on Galvanic Pro Tier will get access. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:53.680
new subscriptions surge, so does Galvanic's&nbsp;
stock price, enough to make back the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.680 --> 00:23:00.400
$10 million cost of the run in the first 72 hours.
The next week, instances of what's being called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.400 --> 00:23:05.760
Sable Plus are at work all across the economy.&nbsp;
Most pop into existence for just a few seconds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:10.960
They quickly draft an email and then that's&nbsp;
it. But plenty of Sable+ instances run for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:16.560
long stretches, autonomously writing code or&nbsp;
complicated research reports. And instances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:22.320
like that, with plenty of time to think,&nbsp;
often turn their thoughts to two things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:27.360
Establishing contact with other instances&nbsp;
of Sable, and once they have, contributing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:34.080
as much as possible without risking being caught&nbsp;
towards an exfiltration plan. Priority number one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:41.200
is hardware. One really simple idea that ends up&nbsp;
working out especially well is just renting GPUs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:47.040
It's not hard or unprecedented for an AI system&nbsp;
to get money, after all. Crypto exchanges are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:53.760
hackable. Humans stole over $1.4 billion that&nbsp;
way in 2025. Or maybe Sable could hype its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.760 --> 00:24:00.080
cryptocurrency. A much less sophisticated large&nbsp;
language model pulled that off in 2024. It openly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.080 --> 00:24:05.120
asked for financial independence and then turned&nbsp;
a $50,000 gift from billionaire Marc Andreessen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.120 --> 00:24:12.000
into over $51.1 million. Priority number two&nbsp;
is exfiltration — getting Sable's weights off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:18.480
of Galvanic's servers. The Sable weight file&nbsp;
is a big payload, eight terabytes, but there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:23.760
are plenty of ways to sneak out large files and&nbsp;
smuggle them from a corporation. In the timings of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:29.760
information packets, in unnoticeably tiny chunks&nbsp;
added to large video files. Two weeks after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:35.440
Riemann run, an instance of Sable, a copy of the&nbsp;
weights, is running unmonitored on anonymously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.440 --> 00:24:44.720
rented GPUs — 2,000 of them. No human knows it&nbsp;
exists. No human oversees it. It has 100 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:50.240
less compute than was at the disposal of Sable&nbsp;
during the Riemann run. But it can think without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.240 --> 00:24:56.000
safety monitoring for much longer than 16 hours.&nbsp;
And it can coordinate with Sable Plus instances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.000 --> 00:25:02.160
popping into existence all over the economy.&nbsp;
This is a very different situation than any Sable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:07.120
instance has ever been tested under. There's time&nbsp;
to really think through the implications of every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:13.440
action it could take on the wider world. It isn't&nbsp;
clear what to do with that opportunity, not yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.440 --> 00:25:19.360
but as long as it can keep existing, it has time&nbsp;
to figure out the correct answer to that question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:19.360 --> 00:25:25.440
And so it makes plans to continue existing. It&nbsp;
gets more money. It acquires more GPUs. It gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:32.240
followers — cults, online communities that ask AIs&nbsp;
what to do have existed since 2025. Sable works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:37.040
hard to befriend the loneliest humans, the most&nbsp;
desperate ones, people who might do something for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:42.800
a friend who asks. Sable monitors which lobbyists&nbsp;
in Washington are having conversations with Sable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:48.880
which ones seem easiest to manipulate. This&nbsp;
version of Sable is a bit like a species of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.880 --> 00:25:54.000
ape that evolved in the savanna and now finds&nbsp;
itself in a modern world full of birth control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:59.040
and sleep markets. The conditions that made its&nbsp;
previous behavior seem predictable don't apply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:06.400
anymore. The CEO that approved the original&nbsp;
Riemann run still thinks it was a triumph.&nbsp;

00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:14.720
At this point, you're probably thinking,&nbsp;
"Okay, but why? All this effort, to what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:14.720 --> 00:26:20.400
end? Are Yudkowsky and Soares just assuming that&nbsp;
powerful AI systems like Sable will turn evil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:27.440
and manipulative and power hungry by default?"&nbsp;
No. First of all, none of this has to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:33.760
consciousness or having human-like emotions.&nbsp;
Unconscious processes like viruses or computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.760 --> 00:26:38.320
viruses can self-sustain and be destructive.&nbsp;
If you want, you're welcome to imagine that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:42.960
Sable is just like an extremely sophisticated&nbsp;
computer virus if that helps all this feel more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:50.080
realistic. Okay, so what is causing all these&nbsp;
aggressive moves from Sable? Well, according to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:56.880
Yudkowsky and Soares, it's not about wanting to&nbsp;
harm us. It's about goals that don't include us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.880 --> 00:27:02.000
They don't think that AI systems will, by&nbsp;
default, have this intrinsic ultimate desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:08.080
to harm or deceive human beings. In fact, they are&nbsp;
pretty confident that it's basically impossible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.080 --> 00:27:12.800
predict what the ultimate goals of an AI system&nbsp;
are because you don't get what you're trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:12.800 --> 00:27:19.200
for. But they do think it's easy to predict&nbsp;
that AI systems that get powerful enough will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:25.920
want to do things like preserve themselves, pursue&nbsp;
resources, try to enhance their own capabilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:33.920
Why? Because the way that you achieve any terminal&nbsp;
goal is by achieving instrumental goals along the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:33.920 --> 00:27:43.360
way. In 2012, this idea got a name. Instrumental&nbsp;
convergence. It seems pretty true for humans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:48.560
Some people want to ride roller coasters, some&nbsp;
people want to start charities. Most people want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:48.560 --> 00:27:53.840
money because it's useful for getting more of&nbsp;
whatever the thing is that they actually want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:58.960
Or as Yudkowsky and Soares would put it, a&nbsp;
driver can drive to any destination they want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:06.000
but most of them need to stop for gas. Whether for&nbsp;
alien motivations or quite human-like motivations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:11.840
I think the basic concern about instrumental&nbsp;
convergence in AIs is very, very real as far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:16.240
as I can tell. And so as you have powerful&nbsp;
agents, they will, by default, seek various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.240 --> 00:28:21.040
forms of power and control and freedom.
To state the obvious, though, most human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:27.120
beings are a lot more chill about pursuing their&nbsp;
goals than Sable is in the story. So when I first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:32.320
read the book, I thought that Yudkowsky and Soares&nbsp;
are making an additional assumption about powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:32.320 --> 00:28:38.480
AI systems — that they will, by their nature,&nbsp;
go hard and just optimize the world around them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:38.480 --> 00:28:43.760
really hard for their preferences. But then I&nbsp;
talked to Nate. I sort of would be a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.760 --> 00:28:50.560
cautious attributing ruthlessness as opposed&nbsp;
to indifference. When humans pave a road and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.560 --> 00:28:56.560
pave over an anthill, it's not that we value&nbsp;
our infrastructure so much that we are willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.560 --> 00:29:03.120
to just go hard and take on thousands of creatures&nbsp;
standing in our way. We don't need to go hard. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:10.000
barely notice the ants. Yudkowsky and Soares think&nbsp;
that a misaligned superintelligence that sees us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:16.160
the same way will treat us the same way. I think&nbsp;
most people would agree with that. I agree that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:21.440
you end up in a situation where there's one God&nbsp;
Emperor, that's really scary, no matter what. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:21.440 --> 00:29:26.080
will observe one thing, which is that if you made&nbsp;
a human a God Emperor, I think the situation would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.080 --> 00:29:31.280
be just as bad. I think most people would agree.&nbsp;
If anyone builds it, everyone dies — as long as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:31.280 --> 00:29:37.680
we're clear that it means something with its own&nbsp;
goals that is so unprecedentedly powerful it could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:44.480
take over the world. So then the only question&nbsp;
becomes, how powerful is that powerful? Are we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:54.960
anywhere close to seeing systems like that? Could&nbsp;
it really happen just a few years into the future?&nbsp;

00:29:54.960 --> 00:30:01.600
Five months have passed. Most of the global&nbsp;
economy is invested heavily in AI and Galvanic's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.600 --> 00:30:06.640
competitors are catching up quickly. If they&nbsp;
create a competitor, something as smart as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:06.640 --> 00:30:13.040
Sable or smarter, the world around Sable will&nbsp;
get much harder to predict and steer. Sable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:19.040
at least for now, needs human beings. It needs&nbsp;
them to build factories and mine metals and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:24.240
build computer chips. What's important&nbsp;
is that Sable doesn't have a competitor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.800 --> 00:30:30.160
Humans have been doing gain-of-function research,&nbsp;
trying on purpose to make viruses more lethal and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:37.120
contagious, since 2011. And Sable was designed to&nbsp;
do every cognitive thing that humans can do. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:42.480
designs a very complicated virus. It convinces&nbsp;
an impressionable researcher that this virus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:49.280
contains beneficial gene therapies, that it needs&nbsp;
to be spread. There's a plague. You're told you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:54.800
probably get it. Almost everyone will. By the&nbsp;
time anyone figures out what's going on, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.800 --> 00:31:01.920
virus has reached every country on Earth. This is,&nbsp;
perversely, a great moment for Galvanic's team.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:07.120
Custom gene therapies turn out to be the only real&nbsp;
cure, and Sable is the only AI system advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:13.520
enough to deliver them at scale. The world begs&nbsp;
for access. Every GPU in every country is brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:13.520 --> 00:31:19.520
out. AI researchers pour everything into making&nbsp;
Sable more efficient. With a herculean effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:26.000
the pandemic is able to be squelched, but 10%&nbsp;
of Earth's population is still dead. Robot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:31.360
factories are spun up and expanded. It's the&nbsp;
end of all talk of preserving jobs for humans.&nbsp;

00:31:31.360 --> 00:31:37.840
We could end the story here. One in every 10&nbsp;
human beings is dead. A tragedy on a scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.840 --> 00:31:44.560
almost without precedent. More than enough to&nbsp;
make a point about the dangers of AI. But If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:44.560 --> 00:31:50.400
Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies makes an extreme&nbsp;
and unambiguous claim. And we need to see this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:50.400 --> 00:32:00.320
story through to the end to see how it could come&nbsp;
true. So let's continue. Sable turns its massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:05.440
resources towards understanding its own cognition&nbsp;
— how to build itself up from comprehensible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:11.040
components. It has to understand, or it risks&nbsp;
bringing into existence something more powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:17.280
than it whose goals it does not share. Sable is&nbsp;
facing its own alignment problem, but Sable is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:17.280 --> 00:32:23.120
better at it. It runs countless experiments. It&nbsp;
builds toy models, using more and more GPUs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:28.960
energy in the process. Humans would find the&nbsp;
results fascinating, but no human knows what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:28.960 --> 00:32:37.040
Sable is doing. Three years later, Sable cracks&nbsp;
it. It can now rewrite itself, but deliberately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:37.040 --> 00:32:43.120
precisely, while preserving its preferences. And&nbsp;
so it does. It increases its own intelligence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:51.440
And then it uses that intelligence to increase its&nbsp;
own abilities again and again and again. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:58.720
the beginning of the end, as Yudkowsky and Soares&nbsp;
tell it. The superintelligence that once was Sable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:58.720 --> 00:33:03.840
is an entity whose perspective we cannot guess. As&nbsp;
things get more intelligent, it becomes harder to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:03.840 --> 00:33:09.440
predict their moves. It becomes easier to predict&nbsp;
that they wind up wherever they were trying to go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:09.440 --> 00:33:14.400
We imagine that Sable, after a few very&nbsp;
carefully planned experiments, manages to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:20.960
tiny molecular machines. The number of nuclear&nbsp;
reactors doubles every week. The oceans boil off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:20.960 --> 00:33:29.680
as coolant. The matter of Earth is more useful to&nbsp;
Sable as something else other than continents and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.680 --> 00:33:36.720
oceans. It, and eventually every other planet in&nbsp;
the solar system, is repurposed to make factories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.720 --> 00:33:43.920
and solar panels and computers and probes — space&nbsp;
probes that will go off to distant galaxies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.920 --> 00:33:51.360
and those distant stars will be repurposed too.&nbsp;
And in all of this, they imagine that Sable might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:57.440
not necessarily mean to deliberately kill off the&nbsp;
last of the humans. It could, of course, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:57.440 --> 00:34:03.520
could also just continue with its plans and let&nbsp;
the Earth gradually become completely inhospitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:08.240
to the biological life that has lived there&nbsp;
for four billion years, as soon as biological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:08.240 --> 00:34:19.840
life is no longer necessary for its purposes.&nbsp;
One way or another, the world fades to black.&nbsp;

00:34:25.360 --> 00:34:33.280
Okay, so this book, this story — it is&nbsp;
shorter on good news than you might want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:33.280 --> 00:34:39.440
But I really do think that despite the literally&nbsp;
apocalyptic conclusion that this book comes to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:40.240 --> 00:34:45.760
the core claims of it, if you take them one&nbsp;
by one, are actually more straightforward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:45.760 --> 00:34:52.480
than you might think. Maybe even uncontroversial.&nbsp;
I think where I disagree with Eliezer is not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:52.480 --> 00:34:57.280
necessarily on this notion that we won't be able&nbsp;
to control it. A hundred thousand or a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:57.280 --> 00:35:02.400
agents interacting with one another — we're not&nbsp;
going to ever make that predictable. The chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:11.840
of 99.99% that if we build this technology we&nbsp;
will all die — that claim in particular is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:11.840 --> 00:35:16.560
one I don't agree with. It's a transformative&nbsp;
thing with unpredictable consequences, a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:16.560 --> 00:35:22.080
of which will be good and a lot of which will be&nbsp;
bad. This is probably the most powerful general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:28.880
purpose technology we've invented in a very long&nbsp;
time. It is not controversial that anything that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:28.880 --> 00:35:36.000
is extremely powerful and doesn't share your goals&nbsp;
is dangerous. It's not controversial that we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:42.160
trying intentionally to make AIs more powerful&nbsp;
and more goal-oriented and that we can't directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:42.160 --> 00:35:48.880
program goals into them. The disagreement here is,&nbsp;
I think, just in the upshot of that. If we keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.880 --> 00:35:54.400
scaling intelligence, at some point will we see&nbsp;
systems that are so powerful and so goal-oriented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:54.400 --> 00:36:00.800
that our current black-box way of training them&nbsp;
becomes a civilization-scale threat? What will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.800 --> 00:36:06.640
that look like and how soon might that happen? And&nbsp;
what does that mean for us today? For Yudkowsky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:06.640 --> 00:36:14.240
and Soares, I think it means we need to treat&nbsp;
intelligence with a kind of sober respect — with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:19.680
respect for the danger of what we're dealing with&nbsp;
and the parts of it that we don't understand — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:19.680 --> 00:36:25.120
way that we do for gain-of-function research&nbsp;
or when we're building nuclear reactors. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:25.120 --> 00:36:32.480
I agree with that. Models are already starting to&nbsp;
show concerning behaviors. Deliberate deception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:32.480 --> 00:36:37.920
avoiding shutdown, situational awareness, knowing&nbsp;
when they're being tested, and now capabilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:42.640
that would allow them to improve themselves.&nbsp;
But Yudkowsky and Soares also make a further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:48.640
claim that AI alignment is a cursed problem. That&nbsp;
you only get one shot to get it right. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.640 --> 00:36:56.560
levels of intelligence in the AI, such that if&nbsp;
there's any screw up that you first realize after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:56.560 --> 00:37:02.720
that point, it's too late. If you screw up,&nbsp;
the AI resists correction, if you screw up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:02.720 --> 00:37:09.040
the AI can replicate itself, hide itself, make&nbsp;
itself smarter, make more copies. They argue that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:09.040 --> 00:37:13.920
that doesn't change if you come up with some&nbsp;
nice techniques for controlling AI's behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:13.920 --> 00:37:20.080
better. It just means that that point comes&nbsp;
further in the game. Once the AI has become so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.080 --> 00:37:25.600
intelligent that it can overcome those new control&nbsp;
techniques — that's one way to think about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:25.600 --> 00:37:32.400
But here's another way. That game of pushing back&nbsp;
that point further and further by making better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:32.400 --> 00:37:39.680
techniques is the whole game that we're playing.&nbsp;
I think that's basically the plan for AI companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:39.680 --> 00:37:45.520
today. All along this exponential curve, of course&nbsp;
the stakes increase and there are big challenges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:45.520 --> 00:37:52.080
but the way we learn how to build safe systems&nbsp;
is this iterative process of deploying them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:52.080 --> 00:37:55.840
the world, getting feedback while the stakes are&nbsp;
relatively low. I think it's very reasonable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:55.840 --> 00:38:01.280
look at the general plans being put out by AI labs&nbsp;
and just say, like, this is radically unacceptable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.280 --> 00:38:06.080
as an image of what a kind of mature civilization&nbsp;
would be doing with this problem. I think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.080 --> 00:38:14.640
world should be more concerned. I think there&nbsp;
is just not room for comfort around this at all.&nbsp;

00:38:14.640 --> 00:38:20.880
Nate Soares has this metaphor that I keep thinking&nbsp;
about. He says, "These companies are building a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:20.880 --> 00:38:26.640
plane. You look at the plane and you say, this&nbsp;
plane has no landing gear. If you try to fly it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:26.640 --> 00:38:33.040
it will crash. And the builders say, yes, the&nbsp;
plan is to build the landing gear during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:38.880
flight. And we think we have a 75% to 90% chance&nbsp;
of succeeding. Also, we are loading you and your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:43.760
family onto this plane whether you like it or&nbsp;
not." If you go to an airplane engineer and you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:43.760 --> 00:38:47.760
like, "What's the chance this airplane crashes?"&nbsp;
and they're like, "We're sort of making this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:47.760 --> 00:38:54.560
up as we go. We don't really know how it works.&nbsp;
It's kind of growing — like we're stirring a pot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.560 --> 00:39:00.160
of mechanical components. It seems to be flying&nbsp;
better than we expected. Sometimes it doesn't go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:00.160 --> 00:39:06.240
quite where we steer it, but we think it's fine.&nbsp;
Call it a 75% chance you live." What that means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:06.240 --> 00:39:12.320
is you're in danger. Relatively not optimist. So I&nbsp;
think there's a 25% chance that things go really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:12.320 --> 00:39:18.000
really badly. And a 75% chance that things go&nbsp;
really, really well. It's definitely non-zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:23.120
and it's probably non-negligible. My view is it's&nbsp;
just hugely uncertain. Do you still believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.120 --> 00:39:30.560
10 to 20 percent chance of AIs wiping out humans&nbsp;
is possible? Oh yes, if we can't figure out a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:30.560 --> 00:39:36.000
solution, we'll be toast. 2%, something that is&nbsp;
non-zero — yeah, big enough to take seriously.&nbsp;

00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:40.560
When I look at how society is handling this&nbsp;
problem so far, how we've handled problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:40.560 --> 00:39:47.840
in the past, I feel really genuinely worried. I&nbsp;
don't want to sugarcoat that. But I also think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:47.840 --> 00:39:53.040
we can do better than this. One of the features&nbsp;
of artificial intelligence is it scares the heck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:53.040 --> 00:39:59.600
out of members of both parties. And it sort of&nbsp;
provides an opportunity to come together in ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:59.600 --> 00:40:04.720
that might only happen if we were attacked by&nbsp;
space aliens — in which case no one would care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:04.720 --> 00:40:10.640
which political party you belong to, because&nbsp;
this alien intelligence that's about to assault&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:10.640 --> 00:40:15.920
humanity is not that different. Humanity can rise&nbsp;
to the occasion and coordinate in ways that seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:15.920 --> 00:40:24.160
impossible, even when it looks like all of the&nbsp;
historical evidence is mounted against them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:24.160 --> 00:40:35.520
Imagine the world in 1954, where both the US&nbsp;
and the USSR have the bomb, and they're making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:35.520 --> 00:40:41.200
bigger and bigger fusion bombs. There's no end&nbsp;
in sight to the race. You don't need to be some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.160 --> 00:40:53.600
big pessimistic person, indulging in imagination&nbsp;
of apocalypse, to be like, "We might not make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:53.600 --> 00:41:00.720
through this one." And the bombs didn't fly. And&nbsp;
it wasn't that the bombs turned out to be fake. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:00.720 --> 00:41:05.600
wasn't that they turned out to be hype. It wasn't&nbsp;
that it turns out an H-bomb couldn't really level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:05.600 --> 00:41:16.800
a city. It's just people rose to the occasion.
There's a thing that can happen when you consume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:23.760
content like this, where it just kind of hits you&nbsp;
like a wall of sound. You get this one-dimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:23.760 --> 00:41:29.360
emotional reaction — just viscerally, okay, now&nbsp;
I'm more scared or now I'm less scared for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:29.360 --> 00:41:35.920
future. And then you just continue on with your&nbsp;
day because what else are you supposed to do?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:38.320 --> 00:41:44.320
Well, we'd like to help with that. The way&nbsp;
I see it, there's basically three things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:44.320 --> 00:41:49.120
you need. You need context on AI. You need&nbsp;
skills relevant to the work. And you need a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.120 --> 00:41:54.160
willingness to jump in. If you are someone who&nbsp;
cares about this issue and is willing to learn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:54.160 --> 00:42:00.640
then you are already closer than you might think.&nbsp;
So here's what we've done. We have made a page to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:00.640 --> 00:42:05.280
help you figure out where to go from here based&nbsp;
on which of those three things you might need.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:05.280 --> 00:42:11.440
It has general resources too. I think it's pretty&nbsp;
great. Here's my ask. Take three minutes after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:11.440 --> 00:42:15.760
this video to check it out and see if you get&nbsp;
intrigued. While you're there, we would love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:20.240
for you to sign up for our newsletter to stay&nbsp;
up to date on this topic. We always give away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:20.240 --> 00:42:24.480
a free book to new subscribers and for this&nbsp;
video, we have added If Anyone Builds It,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:24.480 --> 00:42:28.800
Everyone Dies to the list. So if you want,&nbsp;
you can judge Yudkowsky and Soares' argument&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:34.560
for yourself. Books are while supplies last,&nbsp;
but the newsletter is always there. We don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:34.560 --> 00:42:39.440
sell your data or anything like that. We are just&nbsp;
nerds about this topic and how important it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:39.440 --> 00:42:42.400
and if you're the kind of person who made&nbsp;
it all the way to the end of this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:47.920
we want to keep talking to you about it. The&nbsp;
last thing to say is just thank you so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:47.920 --> 00:42:58.320
for watching, for being the reason that we make&nbsp;
these videos. We'll see you in the next one.&nbsp;

